\section{Where files are kept}{

qesR keeps each downloaded file under the name
\verb{<file_id>-<md5>.<ext>}, taken from its catalog entry, after checking it
against the catalog md5. Where depends on the option \code{qesR.cache} (or the
environment variable \code{QESR_CACHE}):
\describe{
\item{\code{"session"} (default)}{a \code{qesR} folder in \code{\link[=tempdir]{tempdir()}}, deleted when
R exits. Nothing is written outside the session's temporary directory.}
\item{\code{"disk"}}{\code{tools::R_user_dir("qesR", "cache")}, kept between
sessions. qesR creates it only when you choose this mode, and says so.}
\item{\code{"none"}}{nothing is kept: each download goes to a new temporary
folder.}
}
Setting \code{qesR.cache_dir} (or \code{QESR_CACHE_DIR}) to an existing directory
keeps files there instead, and implies \code{"disk"} unless \code{qesR.cache} says
otherwise. qesR then works in a \code{qesR} subfolder that it creates and marks,
and never touches the other files of that directory.

A file you download yourself in a browser (for example when a server
refuses automated requests; see \link{qesR-package}) can be put in the cache
under the name the error message gives: qesR uses it once its md5 matches
the catalog.
